1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:02,176
Some numbers are withheld.

2
00:00:02,176 --> 00:00:03,933
Your questions shouldn't be.

3
00:00:03,933 --> 00:00:11,024
In this College Scorecard release, seventy point eight percent of program records suppress their five year earnings.

4
00:00:11,024 --> 00:00:16,963
Withheld makes that missing information visible, then shows what a model can cautiously estimate.

5
00:00:18,463 --> 00:00:25,188
This is a working research prototype built by Rishik Rontala for the Global Innovation Build Challenge.

6
00:00:25,188 --> 00:00:28,256
Search more than thirty five hundred colleges.

7
00:00:28,256 --> 00:00:32,500
Let's open Rutgers and explore an aerospace engineering program.

8
00:00:34,000 --> 00:00:38,677
The teal label matters: this is a model estimate, not an official outcome.

9
00:00:38,677 --> 00:00:48,348
The middle estimate is about ninety four thousand dollars, with a target eighty percent interval from roughly eighty eight to one hundred five thousand.

10
00:00:48,348 --> 00:00:52,709
The interval describes a program median, not an individual's salary.

11
00:00:54,209 --> 00:00:56,139
Open the evidence explanation.

12
00:00:56,139 --> 00:01:00,963
The model uses public program attributes and comparable training programs.

13
00:01:00,963 --> 00:01:08,232
When there are fewer than thirty training peers in the same field and credential, Withheld declines to estimate.

14
00:01:08,232 --> 00:01:11,191
Other kinds of missing data stay unavailable.

15
00:01:12,691 --> 00:01:16,678
The calculator turns a hypothetical loan into monthly terms.

16
00:01:16,678 --> 00:01:20,997
Change the amount and rate, and the payment updates immediately.

17
00:01:20,997 --> 00:01:26,844
Twelve thousand dollars over ten years at zero interest is one hundred dollars a month.

18
00:01:26,844 --> 00:01:30,632
This is an illustration, not a borrowing recommendation.

19
00:01:32,132 --> 00:01:37,838
Now add this program to a comparison, then find a published computer science outcome.

20
00:01:37,838 --> 00:01:40,927
Published observations keep a distinct label.

21
00:01:40,927 --> 00:01:46,297
The interface never quietly replaces an official value with a model prediction.

22
00:01:47,797 --> 00:01:53,418
Compare programs side by side while keeping their sources and uncertainty visible.

23
00:01:53,418 --> 00:01:59,107
An editable benchmark is only your reference, not a federal eligibility threshold.

24
00:01:59,107 --> 00:02:04,523
Export the comparison as a CSV with the source labels and release date intact.

25
00:02:06,023 --> 00:02:09,730
The methodology page shows the evidence behind the design.

26
00:02:09,730 --> 00:02:14,780
Institution families are separated into training, calibration and test groups.

27
00:02:14,780 --> 00:02:24,241
On eight thousand seven hundred twenty three held out published outcomes, the model's average absolute error is about nine thousand thirty dollars.

28
00:02:25,741 --> 00:02:29,624
That improves on the field and credential median baseline.

29
00:02:29,624 --> 00:02:37,122
But the intervals cover only seventy seven point six percent of test outcomes against an eighty percent target.

30
00:02:37,122 --> 00:02:39,665
Coverage is lower for small programs.

31
00:02:39,665 --> 00:02:43,213
Both the improvement and the shortfall are reported.

32
00:02:44,713 --> 00:02:53,060
Under the hood, three Light G B M quantile models learn from field, credential, state, institution type and completions.

33
00:02:53,060 --> 00:02:56,607
Separate calibration data expands their intervals.

34
00:02:56,607 --> 00:03:04,745
A frozen, reproducible pipeline exports the results, and the browser searches them without an account or a paid API.

35
00:03:06,245 --> 00:03:13,323
The most important limitation is simple: we cannot test against outcomes the government has not published.

36
00:03:13,323 --> 00:03:15,126
Suppression is not random.

37
00:03:15,126 --> 00:03:21,069
Performance on published programs does not establish reliability on suppressed programs.

38
00:03:21,069 --> 00:03:26,277
These estimates do not recover private data or automate real money decisions.

39
00:03:27,777 --> 00:03:33,047
Withheld brings the question, the evidence and the uncertainty into the same view.

40
00:03:33,047 --> 00:03:38,253
The source code, model files, data provenance and validation results are public.

41
00:03:38,253 --> 00:03:43,009
Explore the live prototype, inspect the work, and keep the gaps in sight.
